./README.md:88:| T2 | CRUD Bookmarks | normal | Core API implementation |
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:1:# Product Specification: Bookmark Manager API
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:5:A REST API for managing bookmarks with folder organization, user authentication, and pagination. Built with TypeScript, Express, and better-sqlite3.
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:11:- **Bookmark**: A saved URL with title, optional description, timestamps
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:12:- **Folder**: A named collection of bookmarks
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:13:- **User**: An authenticated account that owns bookmarks and folders
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:20:#### Bookmarks (after auth: scoped to authenticated user)
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:21:- `POST /bookmarks` — Create a bookmark (requires: url, title)
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:22:- `GET /bookmarks` — List all bookmarks (paginated after T6)
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:23:- `GET /bookmarks/:id` — Get single bookmark
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:24:- `PUT /bookmarks/:id` — Update a bookmark
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:25:- `DELETE /bookmarks/:id` — Delete a bookmark
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:30:- `GET /folders/:id` — Get folder with its bookmarks
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:32:- `DELETE /folders/:id` — Delete folder (bookmarks become unfoldered)
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:44:- Bookmark `url` must be a valid URL
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:45:- Bookmark `title` must be non-empty string
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:54:- `GET /bookmarks?page=1&limit=20`
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:55:- Response shape: `{ data: [...], page: 1, limit: 20, total: 45 }`
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:56:- Default: page=1, limit=20
./PRODUCT_SPEC.md:57:- Max limit: 100
./docs/stories/US-010-folder-sharing-permissions.md:8:Allow folder owners to share folders read-only with another registered user by email. Shared users can inspect the shared folder and its bookmarks, but all mutations inside that folder remain owner-only.
./docs/stories/US-010-folder-sharing-permissions.md:15:- A shared user can read the shared folder and its bookmarks.
./docs/stories/US-010-folder-sharing-permissions.md:16:- A shared user receives 403 for write attempts against shared-folder bookmarks or when creating a bookmark in the shared folder.
./docs/stories/US-010-folder-sharing-permissions.md:17:- Users without ownership or an active share receive 404 for private folders/bookmarks.
./docs/decisions/0010-folder-sharing-read-only-authorization.md:8:Folder sharing introduces a second access mode for folders and bookmarks. Existing access checks were owner-only and mostly encoded directly in SQL through `user_id = ?` predicates.
./docs/decisions/0010-folder-sharing-read-only-authorization.md:12:Folder sharing is modeled as explicit read-only grants in a `folder_shares` table. A user may read a folder when they own it or have an active share for it. A user may write folders, bookmarks, tags, import data, and bookmark-tag assignments only when they own the affected data.
./docs/decisions/0010-folder-sharing-read-only-authorization.md:14:Shared users receive 403 for write attempts against resources they can identify through an active share. Users without ownership or a share receive 404 for private folders/bookmarks to avoid leaking private data.
./docs/decisions/0010-folder-sharing-read-only-authorization.md:20:- Tags on shared bookmarks are rendered from the owner account because bookmark tags belong to the owner.
